Neon containers: blank pages stay in their container.

A page without a site (about:blank) commits in a SiteInstance without a site,
and such a SiteInstance always reports the default storage partition. A blank
tab opened in a container, a tab of a container left on about:blank (the new
tab page, then about:blank typed), or a restored blank tab therefore ran in the
default partition, and its next navigation either left the container or hit
the CHECK in BrowsingInstance::ComputeSiteInfoForURL.

- content: a BrowsingInstance fixed to a non-default storage partition assigns
  a site to such pages, as it already does for <webview> guests, so the page
  keeps the partition (browsing_instance.cc, one condition).
- tab_util: a new tab on a page without a site in a container gets a
  SiteInstance for the empty URL fixed to the container, as noopener windows
  do; a restored blank tab with no site in its history gets the same.
- A new tab whose source page is in the default partition (an extension page
  in the global window) opens where a tab with no source page would, in the
  visible space, instead of in the default partition.

